## 2. Class definition

**Label space:** {{LABEL_SPACE}}
<!-- LABEL_SPACE: list the classes as a fixed set. E.g.
     "{Relevant, Not Relevant}" or "{Bug, Feature, Question, Other}".
     v1 supports binary and multi-class only (DESIGN.md §7.1). -->

**Class definitions** (one paragraph per class, with positive and
negative examples — generic, not real data):

- **{{CLASS_1_NAME}}:** {{CLASS_1_DEFINITION}}
- **{{CLASS_2_NAME}}:** {{CLASS_2_DEFINITION}}
<!-- Add additional CLASS_N_NAME / CLASS_N_DEFINITION rows as needed.
     Filled during consultation; the designer surfaces ambiguity by
     asking for examples that the user might disagree about. -->

**Known borderline cases:**
{{BORDERLINE_NOTES}}
<!-- BORDERLINE_NOTES: places the user already knows the boundary is
     fuzzy. Surfaces baseline-quality risks before Phase 1 labeling.
     "None known" is a valid answer but should prompt skepticism. -->

---

## 3. Success criteria

**Production decision rule** (the threshold or rule the prompt's
output drives):
{{DECISION_RULE}}
<!-- DECISION_RULE: e.g. "any ticket classified Billing routes to the
     billing team; everything else stays in general queue." This is
     what the metric must support, not the metric itself. -->

**Headline success criterion** (the single number the user cares
about most):
{{HEADLINE_CRITERION}}
<!-- HEADLINE_CRITERION: e.g. "F1 ≥ 0.85 on test", "recall ≥ 0.95
     with precision ≥ 0.6". Drives metric choice in §4. -->

**Acceptable trade-offs:**
{{TRADEOFF_NOTES}}
<!-- TRADEOFF_NOTES: what the user is willing to give up. E.g.
     "willing to accept 5% precision drop for 10% recall gain because
     missed billing tickets cost more than misrouted ones." -->

---

## 4. Metric

**Selected metric:** {{METRIC_NAME}}
<!-- METRIC_NAME: one of {F1, balanced_accuracy, precision_at_recall,
     recall_at_precision, custom}. Output of metric-design sub-skill
     (DESIGN.md §6 step 3). -->

**Metric rationale** (why this metric, derived from the task
economics in §3):
{{METRIC_RATIONALE}}
<!-- METRIC_RATIONALE: the metric-design sub-skill's reasoning. Must
     reference §3's decision rule and trade-offs. -->

**Independence check** (per DESIGN.md §5; required):
{{METRIC_INDEPENDENCE_NOTE}}
<!-- METRIC_INDEPENDENCE_NOTE: confirmation that the metric is
     computable independently of the model being optimized. "F1 vs
     ground truth labels — model-agnostic" is fine. "GPT-4 judges
     the prompt's outputs" is forbidden in v1 (DESIGN.md §7.1
     multi-judge subjective metrics). -->

